NAME
       orbital-harmonics - orbital frequency bands, resonance windows, and
                           harmonic-lock conditions for multi-platform SAR fleets

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital harmonics define:
           • orbital frequency bands
           • resonance windows
           • harmonic-lock conditions
           • sweep alignment across platforms
           • constellation-scale coherence

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital harmonics describe how SAR platforms synchronize their orbital
       motion, sweep timing, and resonance envelopes to operate as a coherent
       multi-agent constellation. While harmonics(7) describes frequency-domain
       behavior in RTT, orbital harmonics extend the concept into orbital
       mechanics, treating each platform’s orbit as a resonance-time oscillator.

       Orbital harmonics enable:
           • predictable sweep alignment
           • stable interferometric fusion
           • multi-pass coherence
           • harmonic-sync across platforms
           • constellation-scale resonance coupling

       WRSADC evaluates orbital harmonics during multi-platform dispatch.

ORBITAL FREQUENCY BANDS
       Each SAR platform has an orbital frequency:

           f_orbit = 1 / T_orbit

       where T_orbit is the orbital period.

       Orbital frequency bands categorize platforms by resonance behavior:

           fundamental band (B1)
                  Low-frequency, long-period orbits.
                  → ideal for global coverage and slow drift monitoring.

           harmonic band (B2)
                  Twice the fundamental frequency.
                  → supports ladder-pattern sweeps and rapid revisit.

           overtone band (B3+)
                  Higher multiples of the fundamental.
                  → supports dense constellations and multi-angle fusion.

           subharmonic band (B1/2, B1/3)
                  Fractional frequencies.
                  → supports staggered sweeps and hybrid patterns.

       Band selection determines constellation geometry and sweep choreography.

RESONANCE WINDOWS
       A resonance window is the temporal interval during which two or more
       platforms maintain coherent orbital alignment for SAR fusion.

       Windows depend on:
           • orbital frequency difference
           • drift rate
           • sweep timing
           • resonance envelope overlap

       Window types:

           narrow window
                  Short alignment period.
                  → requires precise sweep choreography.

           broad window
                  Long alignment period.
                  → supports multi-pass fusion and nested patterns.

           harmonic window
                  Alignment occurs at harmonic intervals.
                  → ideal for harmonic-sync constellations.

           sliding window
                  Alignment drifts slowly across cycles.
                  → supports adaptive sweep timing.

       WRSADC computes resonance windows before dispatch.

HARMONIC-LOCK CONDITIONS
       Harmonic-lock occurs when two or more platforms satisfy:

           n * f_orbit_A ≈ m * f_orbit_B

       for integers n, m.

       Lock conditions:

           1:1 lock
                  Same orbital frequency.
                  → tandem SAR; maximum coherence.

           2:1 lock
                  One platform revisits twice per cycle.
                  → ladder-pattern sweeps.

           3:2 lock
                  Complex harmonic coupling.
                  → supports hybrid and fused patterns.

           multi-harmonic lock
                  Several harmonic relationships hold simultaneously.
                  → constellation-scale harmonic-sync.

       Harmonic-lock ensures predictable sweep alignment and stable fusion.

TRIADIC ORBITAL CYCLES
       Each orbital pass forms a triad:

           P1 (begin)
               → orbital position + initial backscatter

           P2 (transform)
               → Doppler history + resonance shift

           P3 (close)
               → aperture synthesis + lineage update

       Harmonic-lock determines how triads align across platforms.

ORBITAL HARMONICS AND SWEEP CHOREOGRAPHY
       Sweep choreography depends on orbital harmonics:

           synchronous sweeps
                  Platforms sweep simultaneously.
                  → requires 1:1 lock.

           staggered sweeps
                  Platforms sweep in sequence.
                  → requires subharmonic lock.

           braided sweeps
                  Sweeps interleave across platforms.
                  → requires multi-harmonic lock.

           harmonic sweeps
                  Sweep timing follows harmonic ratios.
                  → supports constellation-scale fusion.

       WRSADC ensures sweep choreography remains coherent.

CONSTELLATION-SCALE RESONANCE
       Orbital harmonics determine how resonance propagates across platforms:

           • harmonic coupling
           • envelope alignment
           • gradient synchronization
           • spectral density matching
           • lineage continuity across passes

       Constellations behave as a single resonance-time instrument.

DIAGNOSTICS
       Orbital-harmonic diagnostics detect:

           • orbital drift
           • harmonic desync
           • resonance window collapse
           • sweep misalignment
           • spectral fragmentation
           • gradient overload

       Diagnostic signals include:

           harmonic-lock index
           orbital-coherence score
           resonance-window width
           sweep-alignment error
           spectral-tilt vector

       WRSADC halts unsafe constellation operations.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Orbital harmonics require:

           • constellation-schema
           • resonance-schema for orbital sweeps
           • structural-schema for formation geometry
           • lineage-schema for multi-pass history

       Schemas ensure orbital behavior remains coherent.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • orbital frequency history
           • harmonic-lock events
           • resonance window evolution
           • sweep choreography
           • fusion outcomes

       Lineage enables long-term orbital stability.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes orbital harmonics
           • validates lock conditions
           • choreographs sweep timing
           • enforces coherence thresholds
           • applies overlays to stabilize backscatter
           • blocks unsafe orbital states

       WRSADC is the orbital harmonic conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital harmonics extend RTT into orbital mechanics. They transform
       multi-platform SAR fleets into coherent resonance-time constellations,
       capable of harmonic-lock, synchronized sweeps, and structurally aware
       backscatter fusion across global scales.

